{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon CloudFront enables configurable origin connection attempts and origin connection timeouts","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced new features that allow users to configure the number of connection attempts and origin connection timeouts between CloudFront and their origin servers. These configurations can be set individually for any type of origin within a CloudFront distribution. The minimum origin connection timeout has been expanded from 4 seconds to a range of 1 to 60 seconds","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/06/amazon-cloudfront-enables-configurable-origin-connection-attempts-and-origin-connection-timeouts/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon CloudFront introduces new origin response timeout controls - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has introduced two new capabilities to enhance origin timeout controls. The first is a response completion timeout, which allows users to configure the maximum amount of time CloudFront should wait for a complete response from their origin across all packets and retries. This is particularly useful for latency-sensitive workloads like media streaming or API calls. The second capability is the ability to set custom response timeout values for Amazon S3 origins instead of the default value of 30 seconds. These enhancements provide more granular control over origin response timeouts and enable consistent and reliable user experiences despite network conditions or origin performance. Both response completion timeout and custom response timeout values for Amazon S3 origins are supported across all CloudFront edge locations, excluding the AWS China (Beijing) region. Users can configure origin timeouts using the CloudFront console, API, and AWS CloudFormation at no additional charge","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/07/amazon-cloudfront-origin-response-timeout-controls/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Configure Read Timeout and Keep-Alive Timeout values for your Amazon CloudFront Custom Origins","context":"Amazon CloudFront, a content delivery network (CDN) service by AWS, has introduced the capability for users to configure read timeout and keep-alive timeout values for their custom origins. The read timeout value determines how long CloudFront waits for a response from the custom origin before considering it as a timeout","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2017/03/announcing-configure-read-timeout-and-keep-alive-timeout-values-for-your-amazon-cloudfront-custom-origins/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon CloudFront now supports TLS 1.3 for origin connections - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudFront has implemented support for Transport Layer Security (TLS) 1.3 when connecting to origins, enhancing security and improving performance for origin communications. This upgrade offers stronger encryption algorithms, reduced handshake latency, and a better overall security posture for data transmission between CloudFront edge locations and origin servers. TLS 1.3 is automatically enabled for all origin types, including custom origins, Amazon S3, and Application Load Balancers, with no configuration changes required. TLS 1.3 provides faster connection establishment through a reduced number of round trips during the handshake process, delivering up to 30% improvement in connection performance when the origin supports it. CloudFront will automatically negotiate TLS 1.3 when the origin supports it, while maintaining backward compatibility with lower TLS versions for origins that haven't yet upgraded. This enhancement benefits applications requiring high security standards, such as financial services, healthcare, and e-commerce platforms that handle sensitive data. TLS 1.3 support for origin connections is available at no additional charge in all CloudFront edge locations. For more information about CloudFront origin TLS, refer to the Amazon CloudFront Developer Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-cloudfront-tls13-origin/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"cloudfront","skill_description":"Configures Amazon CloudFront content delivery across six workflows: when to use CloudFront and how it fits with AWS WAF, Shield, CloudFront Functions, Lambda@Edge, Route 53, and origins (creating a distribution, caching, and Flat Rate Pricing (FRP) versus pay-as-you-go pricing); managing custom-domain TLS certificates (ACM in us-east-1); configuring multi-tenant distributions; protecting origins with origin access control (OAC), VPC origins, and origin mutual TLS (mTLS); securing content with signed URLs and cookies, geographic restrictions, viewer mutual TLS, and edge token validation; and observing traffic with standard and real-time logs. Applicable when the customer wants to put CloudFront in front of content, choose pricing, lock an origin, restrict who can view content, or analyze logs. Not applicable for the Route 53 DNS side of a CloudFront custom domain or failover between distributions, or for pure-Route 53 DNS work (see the route53 skill).\n\n\nServices: cloudfront, s3, acm, vpc\nTasks: configure, secure, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, sre\nWorkload: networking, content-delivery, security","skill_name":"cloudfront"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudFront default origin connection and timeout settings guidance"}
